The rotation breaks the order of the whole array, but one side of the middle is still sorted. I check that sorted side first. If `k` fits inside its value range, the search stays there. Otherwise, the other side is used.

For example, with `[4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2]` and `k = 0`, the first middle value is `7`. The left part is sorted, but `0` is not in its range, so the left pointer moves past `7`. The next checks reach index `4`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int search(vector<int> &nums, int k) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (nums[mid] == k) {
                return mid;
            }

            if (nums[left] <= nums[mid]) {
                if (nums[left] <= k && k < nums[mid]) {
                    right = mid - 1;
                } else {
                    left = mid + 1;
                }
            } else {
                if (nums[mid] < k && k <= nums[right]) {
                    left = mid + 1;
                } else {
                    right = mid - 1;
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

The empty array and a one-element array are covered by the loop bounds. The input array is not changed.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`

Space complexity: `O(1)`

A direct scan is enough here. The array is rotated, but that does not change the fact that each position can be checked against `k`. The first matching position is returned, and `-1` means the value is missing.

