Time complexity: `O(n)`

Space complexity: `O(1)`

This uses the same sorted-half observation in a recursive form. At each call, the middle value is checked, then one half is removed. The recursion stops when the range becomes empty.

For `[5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2, 3]`, if the left half is sorted but the target is outside its bounds, the recursive call is made only for the right half.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    int findValue(vector<int> &nums, int k, int left, int right) {
        if (left > right) {
            return -1;
        }

        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (nums[mid] == k) {
            return mid;
        }

        if (nums[left] <= nums[mid]) {
            if (nums[left] <= k && k < nums[mid]) {
                return findValue(nums, k, left, mid - 1);
            }
            return findValue(nums, k, mid + 1, right);
        }

        if (nums[mid] < k && k <= nums[right]) {
            return findValue(nums, k, mid + 1, right);
        }
        return findValue(nums, k, left, mid - 1);
    }

public:
    int search(vector<int> &nums, int k) {
        return findValue(nums, k, 0, static_cast<int>(nums.size()) - 1);
    }
};
```

The array is read only. The method also handles an empty input because the initial right index is `-1`.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`

Space complexity: `O(log n)` because of the recursive call stack.

Instead of checking the sorted half with several range conditions, this version changes only the value used for comparison. If the middle value and `k` belong to different sides of the rotation, the middle value is treated like infinity or negative infinity. The array itself stays untouched.

That lets the loop behave like an ordinary binary search. The comparison with `nums[0]` tells which side each value belongs to.

```python
class Solution:
    def search(self, nums, k):
        if not nums:
            return -1

        left = 0
        right = len(nums) - 1
        first = nums[0]

        while left <= right:
            mid = left + (right - left) // 2
            middle_value = nums[mid]

            same_side = (middle_value >= first) == (k >= first)
            if not same_side:
                if k >= first:
                    middle_value = float("inf")
                else:
                    middle_value = float("-inf")

            if middle_value == k:
                return mid
            if middle_value < k:
                left = mid + 1
            else:
                right = mid - 1

        return -1
```

For an array with one item, the first comparison either returns index `0` or leaves the range empty. A missing target also ends when `left` passes `right`.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`

Space complexity: `O(1)`

This version finds the rotation point before searching. The smallest value is the start of the second sorted section. After that, a normal binary search can be run on the section that could contain `k`.

A non-rotated array simply has its pivot at index `0`, so it works without a separate special case.

```python
class Solution:
    def search(self, nums, k):
        if not nums:
            return -1

        left = 0
        right = len(nums) - 1

        while left < right:
            mid = left + (right - left) // 2
            if nums[mid] > nums[right]:
                left = mid + 1
            else:
                right = mid

        pivot = left

        if nums[pivot] <= k <= nums[-1]:
            left = pivot
            right = len(nums) - 1
        else:
            left = 0
            right = pivot - 1

        while left <= right:
            mid = left + (right - left) // 2
            if nums[mid] == k:
                return mid
            if nums[mid] < k:
                left = mid + 1
            else:
                right = mid - 1

        return -1
```

Dry run: for `[6, 7, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]`, the pivot becomes `2`. If `k` is `4`, the second sorted section is selected and searched normally.

Time complexity: `O(log n)` for finding the pivot and searching.

Space complexity: `O(1)`.

